        private static readonly string[] HuntressEn = new string[] {
            @"I. Impossible Footprints

Since landing, I had been looking for information about the UES Contact Light. I found abandoned structures, battle damage and supplies whose age I could not establish. Commando and the others had landed in separate sectors. Captain interrupted my search with a broadcast: a secured position had been established, and scattered teams were asked to report their findings. I confirmed I was operational and retained instructions to request route references. My search was not over. Fresh shoeprints crossed the vegetation, with impossible leaps and abrupt changes in direction. They did not belong to an injured person or someone trying to hide. I followed them toward abandoned structures, where a stranger was swinging a bat and celebrating his victories. He failed to notice something approaching from behind.",
            @"II. The Noisiest Stranger on the Planet

I had a clear shot, but the stranger needed to lower his head first. I waited for him to obey before releasing the arrow. The creature fell. I checked whether he was hurt and examined his equipment. He wore none of our expedition markings. I asked whether he was connected to the missing ship, but his account of a research contract he had barely read explained little. He introduced himself as Scout. His extraordinary tracks matched those I had followed. When I resumed my investigation, he insisted on accompanying me. I agreed provided he did not interfere. Judging by how much he talked, that would be difficult.",
            @"III. A Race Nobody Agreed To

Scout moved with exceptional speed, but confused speed with safety. I advised him to skirt a patch of thorny plants. He turned it into a race nobody had accepted. Seconds later he was tangled in branches. I cut him free and checked he could walk. Afterward he paid more attention. While I examined tracks between rocks, a creature approached from my flank. Scout reacted first, striking it with his bat and giving me time to fire. His speed and combat instincts were genuine. I acknowledged that. He seemed delighted by the smallest compliment.",
            @"IV. A Distress Call

My communicator picked up a distress call from a damaged installation. The speakers could be survivors of the Contact Light, members of our expedition or another group entirely. Scout stopped joking when he heard people were trapped. From high ground we saw creatures surrounding a platform and researchers stuck behind a closed door. The release panel lay across the courtyard. I assigned Scout the crossing while I covered him with arrows. He used obstacles for cover, reached the panel and opened the door. When one researcher fell, he ran back to help without waiting for instructions. For once he did not seem concerned with whether anyone had watched.",
            @"V. Those Left Behind

A damaged evacuation vehicle began loading researchers. Someone suddenly realized a woman remained trapped inside. Scout headed back and I followed to cover him. My arrow broke the lock and he lifted the door. We got her out, but another attack had forced the vehicle to take off; its damaged landing system could not bring it back. We found defensible shelter, treated her injuries and transmitted our coordinates. A recovery patrol arrived. I verified that it could care for her and that the others were safe. Her identity and any link to the Contact Light still needed checking. Scout complained about another missed ride, but immediately helped when I asked. He had gone back for a stranger at considerable risk. I told him that mattered.",
            @"VI. A Direction, Not a Race

After reporting the rescue, I requested navigation references through Captain’s channel. The direction matched several landmarks I knew. Scout and I climbed to high ground and spotted lights, buildings and signs of traffic. I had never visited that place, but the reference points matched. I showed him the least exposed route. He left convinced he would be first to arrive. I needed to continue searching: the Contact Light remained missing and the researchers’ identities needed verification. I watched him descend. Moments later he shouted to ask which way to turn. I answered and made sure he took the correct path. Then I resumed my work. I had met a man who could cross enormous distances in seconds. I hoped he would eventually learn where to run.",
        };
        private static readonly string[] ScoutEn = new string[] {
            @"I. The Contract I Should Have Read

I am Scout. Fastest guy you are ever gonna meet. I survived bullets, monsters and even a radiation accident at a power station. Some fancy stranger offered me experimental treatment and a reconnaissance job. The treatment made me faster and stronger; the contract had about a million pages. I read the money part. Next thing I know, the job is on another planet. My transport dumped me among monsters, broken machines and nowhere to eat. The radio was useless too. At least I still had my bat and scattergun. After beating up a few creatures near some abandoned buildings, I practiced my swings and congratulated myself. I did not hear the thing sneaking up behind me. I did hear a woman speak.",
            @"II. The Girl with the Bow

I ducked on instinct. An arrow whistled overhead and a monster dropped behind me. There she was: hood, bow, and that look people get when they are working while I am fooling around. She was pretty, too. She reminded me of Miss Pauling, not because they looked alike, but because somehow one glance made me feel like I had said something dumb. I always tried to impress Pauling and made a mess of it. This would be different. The woman asked about my transport and some Contact Light I had never heard of. I told her about my contract, or what little I knew about it. Her name was Huntress. When she resumed her search for missing people, I insisted on tagging along. She would see what I could do soon enough.",
            @"III. The Race I Almost Won

Huntress knew her way around, but kept taking these huge detours. I saw a straight line through some thornbushes and proposed a race. She refused, so I ran anyway. For about two seconds I was amazing. Then a branch caught my bag and I got tangled up. Rigged course. She cut me free without laughing and checked I could walk. That was somehow worse. Later I saw a monster coming at her flank while she examined some tracks. I jumped in, knocked it over with my bat and she finished it. Teamwork! She said I was fast. Finally! She also said I was loud, which I counted as a second record.",
            @"IV. Nobody Gets Left Behind

Huntress’s communicator started coughing up a voice calling for help. People were trapped. We could not leave them there. She wanted to scout first; for once that sounded smart. From above we saw researchers behind a locked door and monsters all around. The control panel was across the courtyard. She asked me to run to it while she covered me. Perfect plan! I ducked attacks, used crates for cover and reached the panel while arrows kept the monsters off me. The door opened. A researcher fell, so I ran back and helped him up. I have seen big tough guys run away when things get ugly. I was not leaving someone who could barely walk. I even forgot to brag.",
            @"V. You Are Seriously Leaving Without Us?

The researchers had a beat-up evacuation vehicle. We helped the injured aboard until somebody remembered a woman still trapped inside. Seriously? I headed back, and Huntress followed. The door was jammed. She shot the locking mechanism, and I lifted it. The woman could hardly walk. When we got outside, the vehicle was already taking off. Stranded again! I yelled until Huntress told me to help our patient. We found shelter, radioed for help and waited for a recovery patrol. The woman was safe, and the others had arrived too. Huntress pointed out that I went back for someone who could not escape alone. Not everybody would, she said. It sounded sincere. Miss Pauling would probably have asked why we failed to check whether anyone was missing first.",
            @"VI. Somewhere Worth Running To

After our rescue report, Huntress asked for directions over the radio. Apparently a captain had set up somewhere safe. A camp! Food, beds and maybe somebody who could explain my contract. We climbed up the rocks. She compared distant mountains, paths and lights with the directions she had received. She seemed to know which way to go. She showed me the route, but she was not coming: still hunting clues about a missing ship. I said goodbye, convinced I would get there first. Great hunter, that woman, though she talked less than I do in ten seconds. We saved people together; that had to count for something. I ran down the hill and found a fork in the road. Small problem. I yelled to confirm left or right. She answered. I took the left path. I am Scout, fastest guy you will ever meet. If that camp was at the end of the trail, I would get there before anybody noticed.",
        };
